course: dictionary
lesson: 13451
title: Housebound
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Housebound describes someone who cannot leave their home for a while, or for a long time. «Mrs Patel is housebound since her hip operation, so her son brings her shopping each week.» Her hip is hurt, so she cannot go out, and she stays at home.
explain: The word can also mean stuck indoors because of weather or rules. «During the storm, the whole village was housebound for three days.» Nobody could go out, because of the storm, and the word tells you that everyone stayed inside.
explain: Look at the clues in the sentence: who is stuck, and why? Ask whether the reason is illness, age, or something temporary like weather. The reason in the sentence tells you which kind of housebound it is.
fact: Housebound means not able to leave your home.
fact: People can be housebound because they are ill, very old, disabled, or looking after someone.
fact: Housebound can also mean stuck indoors for a while because of bad weather, an injury, or rules such as a lockdown.
fact: Charities and churches often visit housebound people and bring them meals or library books.
fact: Housebound is an adjective, and it is usually not used with «more» or «most».
exercise: «After her fall, Grandad was housebound for six weeks.» What does housebound mean? | Fond of cleaning | *Not able to leave home | Living in a big house | Very happy at home
exercise: «The volunteers bring meals to the housebound neighbours.» Who are the volunteers helping? | Children at the school | Shopkeepers on the high street | People who love to travel | *People who cannot leave their homes
exercise: «The snow was so deep that the family was housebound all weekend.» Why were they housebound? | *The heavy snow stopped them going out | Their house was too small | They were too tired to walk | They wanted to stay in bed
test: «Ben's gran is housebound, so the library posts her books each month.» Why can't gran go to the library herself? | Her library is closed on Sundays | She has moved to another town | *She is not able to leave home | She does not like reading
test: Which situation would make someone housebound? | *A broken leg that stops them walking far | Choosing to go to the park | Buying a new bike | A school trip to the zoo
exam: «Since the lockdown began, Mia has been housebound and misses her friends.» What does housebound mean here? | *Stuck at home because of rules, and unable to go out | Happy to stay in her room all day | Living in a different house from her friends | Very tired from too much school
